<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         THE "FIRE" DESIGN LAYER — token half.

         Extracted from the eleven HTML mockups the owner commissioned
         (`…/Downloads/fire/New Menu/*.dc.html`). It is a SECOND, self-contained
         visual vocabulary that lives ALONGSIDE the app's existing theme — it
         redefines nothing. Every key here is prefixed `Fire`, so the editor,
         the pedalboard, the dialogs and the eight settings tabs keep the look
         they have until a surface explicitly opts in.

         Two deliberate departures from how the rest of the theme works:

         1. THESE COLOURS ARE THEMEABLE AS OF 2026-09-20, and this note said the
            opposite until then. Every literal `SolidColorBrush` in
            `FireTokens/Palette.axaml` now names a `Color` token of its own
            (`FireGround` → `FireGroundColor`), the shape `ColorTokens.axaml` +
            `Resources/ThemeSurfaces.axaml` already used, so a write through
            `ThemeService` (which sets `*Color` keys) reaches the Fire layer —
            i.e. every Play Mode screen — instead of stopping at it. The UI
            rewrite needs that: a design proposal cannot be a theme while the
            surfaces it must recolour are literals
            (`docs/agents/ui/target-architecture.md`). The art direction is
            still the DEFAULT; what changed is that a theme can now replace it.
            The amp-rig identities (`AmpOrange*`/`Rig*`), the six string colours
            and the podium metals stay fixed on purpose — they are materials and
            identities, not palette.
         2. THE SIZE/RADIUS/SPACING LADDERS ARE COMPLETE AND SELF-CONTAINED.
            Several mockup steps (9.5, 10.5, 11.5, 12.5, 13.5, 22, 26, 58 px;
            radius 9/11/12/16/18/20) do not exist on the app's scales. Rather
            than mix two ladders — which is how a screen ends up half-converted
            — a Fire surface uses ONLY `FireSize*` / `FireRadius*` /
            `FirePad*` / `FireGap*`.

         TYPE RULES (do not paraphrase them per screen):
           • Chakra Petch  → display: screen titles, big figures, the wordmark,
                             primary-button captions. Bold (700) except the
                             occasional SemiBold segment caption.
           • Space Grotesk → every piece of running UI text: row titles (600),
                             descriptions (400), button labels (600).
           • IBM Plex Mono → eyebrows, figures, metadata, tags, status text.
                             ALWAYS uppercase, always letter-spaced.

         THE `Padding`/`Margin`/`BorderThickness` TRAP APPLIES HERE TOO:
         `FireGap*` and `FirePad*` are `Thickness`, `FireSpace*` and `FireSize*`
         are `x:Double`. Handing a double token to a Thickness property throws
         `InvalidCastException` when the control attaches, from a clean build.
         Pinned in both directions by `DotCharter.Tests/UI/ResourceTokenTypeTests`.

         THE SPEC'S TOKEN NAMES → THE KEYS HERE. `Implementation Spec.dc.html`
         (§01 Colour) names twenty tokens. Most already had a home under a
         different, JOB-shaped name and were NOT duplicated under the spec's
         name — reading the table below is how you find them:

           Surface / base         → FireGroundScreen        #111317
           Surface / chrome       → FireSurfaceChrome       #15181D
           Surface / chrome alt   → FireSurfaceChromeAlt    #171A20
           Surface / panel        → FireSurfaceCard         white .025
           Surface / glass        → FireRailFill  (+ FireGlassInset for the
                                     spec's `inset 1px white .03`)
           Surface / raised       → FireSurfaceField        white .04
           Border  / hairline     → FireBorder              white .08
           Border  / strong       → FireBorderStrong        white .12
           Text    / primary      → FireTextPrimary         #E8EBEF
           Text    / secondary    → FireTextSecondary       #AEB6C1
           Text    / tertiary     → FireTextMuted           #929BA8
           Text    / quaternary   → FireTextDim             #7A8390
           Text    / disabled     → FireTextFaint           #687080
           Accent  / violet       → FireAccent              #7AB6FF
           Accent  / violet mid   → FireAccentStrong        #5E9FF2
           Accent  / violet deep  → FireAccentDeep          #3C7BD1
           Accent  / violet tint  → FireAccentBright        #A8CEFF
           …/ violet text on fill → FireAccentPale          #CFE2FF
           Signal  / amber        → FireWarning             #F0A54A
           Signal  / green        → FireSuccess             #4ADE80
           Signal  / red          → FireDanger              #EF6F6F

         Note `FireSurfaceRaised` (white .03) is NOT the spec's "Surface /
         raised" — it predates the spec and names the mockups' list-row wash.
         The spec's raised is `FireSurfaceField`.

         FIXED IDENTITIES. The six string colours and the eight gear-category
         colours are IDENTITIES, not palette entries: a string is that colour on
         the highway, in the gutter, in the tuner and on a chord chart, and a
         gear category is that colour on the board, in the gear list and on a
         tone chip. They may never be reassigned, re-ordered or "harmonised"
         with the accent hue. Pinned exactly by
         `DotCharter.Tests/UI/FireTokenValueTests`.

         The component styles that consume these live in `Styles/Fire.axaml`;
         the written-down system is `docs/agents/ui/fire-design-system.md`.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->


    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         THE LADDERS THEMSELVES, BY SECTION. Every `x:Key` this file used to
         declare inline now lives in `Styles/FireTokens/`, sliced along the
         section banners it already carried and moved byte for byte. Each part
         is a `<Styles>` carrying nothing but its own `<Styles.Resources>` —
         the shape this file itself has always had.

         ORDER IS THE CONTRACT. A dictionary merged later wins, so these
         includes stand in the exact order the sections stood in the one file.
         Every key is UNIQUE (a duplicate `x:Key` crashes the app at
         `App.Initialize()`), so nothing depends on the tie-break today —
         which is precisely why a shuffle would be silent.

         The rule the split does NOT relax: `Space*` are `x:Double` and
         `Pad*`/`Gap*` are `Thickness`, and `DynamicResource` does not
         convert. They are in the same part on purpose, so the two ladders
         are still read side by side.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <Styles.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <ResourceInclude Source="avares://DotCharter.Tone.UI/Styles/FireHardwareTokens.axaml" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Styles.Resources>
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://DotCharter.Tone.UI/Styles/FireTokens/Palette.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://DotCharter.Tone.UI/Styles/FireTokens/Ladders.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://DotCharter.Tone.UI/Styles/FireTokens/PopoverTokens.axaml" />

</Styles>
